[R0365] year=2026 | venue=Advanced Intelligent Systems | type=Article
TITLE: OntoLogX: Ontology-Guided Knowledge Graph Extraction From Cybersecurity Logs With Large Language Models
KEYWORDS: autonomous agents; cyber threat intelligence; knowledge graphs; large language models; ontologies; Cybersecurity; Intelligent agents; Intelligent systems; Interoperability; Iterative methods; Knowledge graph; Network security; Semantics; Cybe threat intelligence; Cyber security; Cyber threats; Graph extractions; Knowledge graphs; Language model; Large language model; Malicious activities; Ontology's; Structure semantic; Autonomous agents; Ontology
ABSTRACT: System logs represent a valuable source of cyber threat intelligence (CTI), capturing attacker behaviors, exploited vulnerabilities, and traces of malicious activity. Yet their utility is often limited by a lack of structure, semantic inconsistency, and fragmentation across devices and sessions. Extracting actionable CTI from logs, therefore, requires approaches that can reconcile noisy, heterogeneous data into coherent and interoperable representations. We introduce OntoLogX, an autonomous AI agent that leverages large language models (LLMs) to transform raw logs into ontology-grounded knowledge graphs (KGs). OntoLogX integrates a lightweight log ontology with retrieval augmented generation and iterative correction steps, ensuring that generated KGs are syntactically and semantically valid. Beyond event-level analysis, the system aggregates KGs into sessions and employs a LLM to predict MITRE ATT&CK tactics, linking low-level log evidence to higher-level adversarial objectives. We evaluate OntoLogX on both public and real-world honeypot datasets, demonstrating robust KG generation across multiple LLMs backends and accurate mapping of adversarial activity to MITRE ATT&CK tactics. Results highlight the effectiveness of the methodology in constructing ontology-compliant KGs, along with their value in extracting actionable CTI. © 2026 The Author(s). Advanced Intelligent Systems published by Wiley-VCH GmbH.

[R0366] year=2026 | venue=Proceedings of 7th International Conference on Intelligent Communication Technologies and Virtual Mobile Networks, ICICV 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: LLM-Orchestrated Multi-Agent Framework for Log-Centric Cyber Threat Detection and Analysis
KEYWORDS: LLM Orchestration; MITRE ATT&CK Framework; Multi-Agent Systems; Threat Intelligence; Anomaly detection; Data mining; Distributed computer systems; Intelligent agents; Learning systems; Network security; Scalability; Signal detection; 'current; Cyber threats; Distributed computing systems; LLM orchestration; MITRE ATT&CK framework; Multiagent framework; Multiagent systems (MASs); Threat detection; Threat intelligence; Threats analysis; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: The current cloud-native and distributed computing systems produce enormous amounts of system logs that are heterogeneous at network, host, and application layers. Despite providing highly useful evidence to distinguish cyber threats, such logs are unstructured, highly variable, and large-scale, making them challenging to analyze manually and limiting the capabilities of traditional Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) systems. Traditional rule-based application detection and monolithic machine learning applications have problems with scalability, contextual knowledge and high rates of false positives in dynamic environment. To address these shortcomings, this paper introduces an LLM-Orchestrated multi-agent system of log-based detection and analysis of cyber threats that includes the generation of structured signals, contextualization of features, and intelligent agent-driven reasoning. The suggested framework initially uses an asynchronous log ingestion mechanism which enables the processing of large volume log streams on a scaled basis. Raw logs are then converted to structured signals by Drain3 based online log template mining and regex-based normalization. The signals are augmented with contextual threat data and past behavioral patterns, and then statistical anomaly detection is performed using z -score analysis. These signals are then mapped to corresponding MITRE ATT&CK techniques and fed to a central orchestration layer which then sends them to specific analytical agents that combine deterministic detection rules with reasoning using large language models to validate threats. The results of experimental assessment performed on 6000 raw logs constituting known and unknown attack cases show that the framework results in detection rates of 98.5 and 92.7 respectively and enhances scalability and reliability in log-based cyber threat detection. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0367] year=2026 | venue=2026 International Wireless Communications and Mobile Computing Conference, IWCMC 2026 | type=Conference paper
TITLE: Agentic SOC: A Hierarchical Swarm-Orchestrated Multi-Agent LLM Architecture for Autonomous OT Cyber Defense
KEYWORDS: 5G/6G Wireless Security; Autonomous Cyber Defense; Hierarchical Multi-Agent Systems; IT-OT Security; Security Operations Center; Swarm Intelligence; 5G mobile communication systems; Architecture; Autonomous agents; Control systems; Cyber Physical System; Distributed computer systems; Embedded systems; Hierarchical systems; Intelligent agents; Intelligent control; Man machine systems; Mobile security; Multi agent systems; Network layers; Network security; Security systems; Wireless networks; 5g/6g wireless security; Autonomous cybe defense; Cyber-defense; Hierarchical multi-agent system; Industrial control systems; IT-operational technology security; Multi agent; Operational technologies; Security operation center; Wireless security; Swarm intelligence
ABSTRACT: The convergence of enterprise IT networks with operational technology (OT), industrial control systems (ICS), and 5G/6G wireless-enabled cyber-physical infrastructures has expanded the attack surface of critical environments, exposing limitations in traditional IT-centric Security Operations Centers (SOCs). IT-OT systems impose strict latency, safety, and availability constraints, while rule-based detection and human-driven triage lead to alert fatigue, delayed response, and fragmented visibility across wired and wireless domains. This paper presents Agentic SOC, a hierarchical swarm-orchestrated multi-agent LLM architecture for autonomous and adaptive cyber defense in IT-OT converged networks. The architecture decomposes SOC operations into three cooperative tiers: a perception layer of distributed agents performing real-time telemetry analysis across IT, OT, and wireless network domains; a reasoning layer executing cross-domain correlation, MITRE ATT&CK (including ICS) mapping, and coordinated mitigation; and a strategic leader agent conducting kill-chain reconstruction, RAG-grounded threat hunting, and adversarial simulation. We implement and evaluate Agentic SOC under a managed security service provider (MSSP) model across four safety-critical scenarios: 6G telecom infrastructure, a power generation plant, a railway control system, and a three-dimensional Security of Air-Ground-Space Integrated Networks (SAGIN) environment. Experimental benchmarking demonstrates broader threat coverage, improved cross-domain correlation, reduced false positives, and a lower mean time to detection and response compared to flat, multi-agent, and rule-based SOC baselines while preserving human-in-the-loop governance. Agentic SOC provides an experimentally validated blueprint for AI-native, self-protecting SOC architectures in next-generation IT-OT cyber-physical systems. © 2026 IEEE.

