TITLE: Multi-dimensional Prompt Expansion: Black-Box DoS Attacks on Multi-agent LLMs
KEYWORDS: adversarial prompt engineering; black-box attacks; large language model multi-agent system; Computational linguistics; Computational methods; Denial-of-service attack; Expansion; Intelligent agents; Iterative methods; Network security; Query processing; Semantics; Trees (mathematics); Adversarial prompt engineering; Application prospect; Black boxes; Black-box attack; Denialof- service attacks; Language model; Large language model multi-agent system; Model multi-agent systems; Multi agent; Multi dimensional; Multi agent systems
ABSTRACT: As an emerging research direction in the field of artificial intelligence, the large language model multi-agent systems (LLM-MAS) demonstrate vast application prospects and developmental potential. However, the open services of LLM-MAS are vulnerable to external threats, especially large language model denial-of-service (DoS) attacks. These attacks aim to degrade service performance and cause resource crashes by generating malicious inputs to exhaust the computational resources of LLM-MAS. In this paper, a red-team DoS generation method suitable for the black-box environment is proposed. The Multi-Dimensional Prompt Expansion (MDPE) Attack is generated by decomposing the initial prompt into a tree structure for domain-specific segmentation and semantic expansion. It employs feedback-based iterative optimization and further injects deceptive length constraints. Experiments demonstrate that our method significantly expands the response content length and prolongs service latency in LLM-MAS, compared to benign queries and baseline approaches. The study investigates the GPU resource consumption and service throughput of this attack in real-world deployments, while validating its transferability and stealthiness across different large language models. Ablation experiments further analyze the contribution of key components to the overall attack performance. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Singapore Pte Ltd. 2026.

[R0369] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Access | type=Article
TITLE: MaID-Agent: A Hybrid Masqueraded Intrusion Detection System Enhanced by Agent
KEYWORDS: adversarial traffic generation; autonomous agents; explainable IDS; fake-normal camouflage; Internet of Things security; Intrusion detection; LLM-based investigation; masquerade attack; multimodal anomaly detection; Anomaly detection; Computer crime; Internet of things; Multi agent systems; Network security; Adversarial traffic generation; Anomaly detection; Explainable IDS; Fake-normal camouflage; Internet of thing security; Intrusion-Detection; Language model; Large language model-based investigation; Masquerade attacks; Model-based OPC; Multi-modal; Multimodal anomaly detection; Traffic generation; Autonomous agents; Intrusion detection
ABSTRACT: The deployment of MQTT/IoT and the emergence of fine-tuned large language models (LLMs) enable a new evasion opportunity for attackers: a masquerade attack that uses a language model to generate fake-normal (F) traffic which mimics the activities of a normal broker and thereby dilutes the malicious (M) fraction seen by IDSs. Our analysis shows that existing single-modality detectors fail at representation in the presence of such camouflage and that this problem cannot be addressed merely through re-training. We formulate the fake-normal threat model and introduce MaID-Agent, a two-tier solution where in Tier-1, a multi-view representation that combines three independent features, temporal (TranAD), payload semantic (FAISS), and character-morphological (CharSVD) using an Adaptive Weighting Network (AWN) trained under an F-aware classification rule that maps both F and M instances into the same positive category. In Tier-2, we call an agent on the thin slice where there is uncertainty, resulting in F/M disambiguation and per-sensor evidence and autonomous actions including {IGNORE, BLOCK_ALERT, UPDATE_DB}. MaID-Agent is validated against seven single-modality IDSs across five seeds with respect to malicious-to-camouflage ratios ranging between 8:8 and 15:1 on MQTTSet and DDMI. While the recall performance of all the baseline detectors monotonically drops with increasing malicious-to-camouflage ratios. TranAD's recall drops to 0.356 (MQTTSet) and 0.200 (DDMI) at ratio 15:1. MaID-Agent restores to 0.986 (MQTTSet) and 0.988 (DDMI). The recovery is due to morphology (MQTTSet) and payload semantic (DDMI) feature, a clear demonstration of the effectiveness of multimodality rather than ensembling. In the Tier-2, the agent fills the decision-to-action gap based on the verdict provided by Tier-1 at sub-100ms amortized per-packet costs. These findings highlight the importance of a combination of multimodal detection with an agent for building robust IDS systems capable of operating under new and emerging adversarial conditions, ultimately paving the way for more resilient IoT security frameworks in the face of evolving threats. © 2013 IEEE.

