ABSTRACT: Traditional cybersecurity technologies have difficulty adapting to the increasing sophistication and numbers of cyberattacks. Typically, traditional technologies use manual interventions and static rules to defend against cyberattacks; therefore, they are limited in their ability to adapt and respond quickly to changing and evolving threats. This research addresses this problem by proposing a Generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) Self-Healing Cybersecurity Framework to facilitate the autonomous detection of threats and recovery from attacks on networks. The proposed framework combines advanced machine learning methods, such as deep learning models, generative adversarial networks (GANs), and reinforcement learning, to create a smart, flexible, and adaptive cybersecurity architecture. This system monitors all your network traffic constantly for unusual activity, uses artificial intelligence technology to identify trends and forecast future threats, and creates simulations of potential attacks through a generative artificial intelligence module to help the system identify new types of threats before they become real. When a threat is identified, the decision engine automatically determines the best way to mitigate the threat without human intervention. Finally, the self-healing recovery process restores normal operation of the network. Based on an experimental evaluation with benchmark cybersecurity datasets, the proposed framework demonstrates a 97.1% detection accuracy (with 96.5% precision and 95.8% recall), significantly outperforming traditional intrusion detection systems. The autonomous recovery mechanism also reduces the time required to recover an entire network by over 70%, contributing to a greater level of resilience within the system. As such, the proposed framework provides a viable and scalable solution to improving cybersecurity within today's network environments. © 2026 IEEE.

[R0378] year=2026 | venue=Computers and Geotechnics | type=Article
TITLE: A novel large language model–inspired transformer framework for reconstructing subsurface soil properties at untested locations from sparse cone penetrometer test data
KEYWORDS: Attention mechanism; Deep learning; Self-supervised learning; Site characterization; Spatial variability of soil; Benchmarking; Computational efficiency; Data accuracy; Learning algorithms; Self-supervised learning; Soil surveys; Soil testing; Soils; Statistical tests; Supervised learning; Uncertainty analysis; Attention mechanisms; Cone penetrometer test; Deep learning; Geotechnical; Language model; Learning frameworks; Site characterization; Spatial variability; Spatial variability of soil; Test data; cone penetration test; machine learning; site characterization; soil mechanics; soil property; spatial variation; Deep learning
ABSTRACT: Accurate characterization of subsurface conditions from cone penetrometer test (CPT) data remains challenging due to inherent soil spatial variability and laterally sparse datasets. While transformers with attention mechanisms have demonstrated strong capability in capturing complex dependencies across various domains, their application for modeling spatial dependencies within CPT data has not been fully explored. Inspired by the training strategies and architecture of large language models, we propose a novel geotechnical self-supervised learning framework (GSSLF) to reconstruct the subsurface soil properties at untested locations from incomplete and sparsely distributed CPT data. GSSLF integrates a self-supervised masking strategy to augment limited training data and employs a tailored transformer-based architecture (GeoFormer) with a modified attention mechanism to adaptively capture spatial dependencies. A heteroscedastic model is also incorporated to provide reliable probabilistic predictions and quantify associated uncertainties. GSSLF is shown to deliver competitive performance with robust accuracy and reliable uncertainty estimation, when compared to other recently developed data-driven methods (GLasso, ASSD-BCS, GeoWarp, and BNN-BO) on three benchmark datasets from literature. It achieves this with high computational efficiency, enabling efficient high-resolution three-dimensional subsurface modeling. Moreover, incorporating relative positional encoding is found to be preferable for attention-based deep learning in limited CPT data scenarios. These findings highlight the potential of GSSLF as a robust and efficient tool for subsurface modeling, facilitating both deterministic and reliability-based geotechnical design and planning. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd

[R0379] year=2026 | venue=IEEE Transactions on Cognitive Communications and Networking | type=Article
TITLE: Generative AI-Empowered Signal Processing for Collaborative Embodied Agents: A Survey on Agentic Security
